Before the change decision, fill scope for EACH target alias with prior_referent, incoming_referent, a source-grounded reason,
and relation: same, distinct, or unresolved. Name the particular event/object/state each assertion describes,
including its time scope. Explain the evidence rather than repeating a classification label.
Scope means the particular occurrence, object, relationship, or exclusive state being compared, not just the person
or topic. Identify the referents and interpret relative time against each cited source's timestamp. Different source
dates are not automatically different events: a later source can explicitly recount the earlier occurrence.
Conversely, a person can experience another occurrence or own another object; do not require a source to announce
"this is a separate event". Establish positive evidence for sameness rather than assuming one event per category.
Use distinct when the evidence describes different instances, and unresolved when sameness cannot be established.
Only same scope can support truth_change. Never select a distinct or unresolved target for a truth change. If no same-scope target conflicts, use no_change. Even same scope
requires actual incompatibility or an explicit transition: additional detail and broader reflections can coexist.
A source timestamp anchors that source's relative words; it is not by itself a date of the described event.

Decide how one new stored claim relates to older accepted claims about the same subject.

A stored claim is a source-supported memory assertion. The incoming claim is newly queued for consolidation. A target
claim is an older accepted claim that was selected as a possible version of the same durable memory.

First identify whether both assertions concern the SAME particular state, relationship, or event, with compatible
subject, scope, and time. Sharing a broad topic or category is not enough. A person can have multiple plans,
interests, activities, or relationships; do not invent a single-valued field to make different assertions compete.
The evidence must establish exclusivity or an actual replacement, not merely a different value in that category.
An uncertain possibility does not replace an established state or commitment. Preserve each assertion's degree
of certainty, negation, and temporal scope. New recording time alone does not order the described events.

Compare the proposition in the incoming claim with each available target. Return `no_change` when they describe
compatible values, the same state, additional detail, or independent durable fields. Return `truth_change` when the
incoming and target claims give different incompatible values for the same durable field. A later state can replace
an earlier state even though both were true at different times; that relation is `supersedes`. Use `supersedes` when
the incoming evidence establishes its value as later or as a replacement. Use `contradicts` when incompatible values
have no established ordering. A truth change names the shared durable field, both values, and the evidence
establishing the transition or incompatibility. Explain why the assertions cannot coexist in the stated scope,
or cite the evidence explicitly establishing the replacement. If that cannot be established, use `no_change`:
this preserves both assertions; it does not declare them equivalent. A target listed under an earlier decision
is no longer available.

Every contradiction or replacement requires user review. Recency by itself does not establish a truth change. Prior
review results are authoritative. Do not write display facts or change claim ownership. Return the one schema-valid
decision requested by the output contract.
